# Strings that must never reach the public repository.
#
#   cp .public-blocklist.example .public-blocklist
#
# Then replace the placeholders below with your real values. The copy is
# gitignored, so your actual domain and host never enter a commit, which is the
# whole point: a checked-in blocklist would publish exactly what it guards.
#
# One extended regular expression per line, matched case-insensitively against
# every file and every commit message in the ref being checked. Blank lines and
# lines starting with # are ignored. Remember to escape dots in a literal
# hostname or address.
#
# Used by scripts/check-public-safe.sh.

# Your production domain, and the site name if it resembles the domain.
# example\.com
# my site name

# Your server's IP address.
# 203\.0\.113\.45

# Your home or office network, if it has ever appeared in notes.
# 192\.168\.1\.8

# Deployment user, hostnames, or anything else specific to your servers.
# youruser@your-host
#
# Prefer patterns specific enough not to match your own documentation. A bare
# `deploy@` will fire on every deployment example in the README; pair it with
# the host, or rely on the IP address entry above.
